  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warmest temperatures of the summer for most this weekend \n   through early next week. Elevated heat risk for those sensitive\n   to heat, especially those without cooling or hydration.\n\n-  Low relative humidity and increased west winds will result in\n   elevated fire weather concerns on Saturday and Sunday,\n   especially in southwest Montana. \n\n-  Monsoon moisture surge will bring clouds, showers, and\n   thunderstorms by Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures will run a few degrees warmer this afternoon (Friday)\nand will continue to warm as we progress into the weekend. Valleys\nacross western Montana can expect daytime highs in the 90s, while\nlower elevation areas like Hells Canyon and the lower Salmon River\nvalleys will reach triple digits. Heat Advisories have been issued\nfor the Hells Canyon area and the lower Salmon River valleys in\nLemhi County, which go into effect tomorrow and remain in place\nthrough Sunday. Moderate heat impacts are expected for valleys\nalong and east of Highway 93, meaning individuals sensitive to\nheat may be affected. In addition to the heat, strong upper-level\nwinds combined with low daytime humidity will create near-critical\nfire weather conditions in southwest Montana, with a Fire Weather\nWatch in place for Sunday. For those recreating outdoors this\nweekend, please prioritize safety: stay consistently hydrated,\ntake frequent breaks in the shade, avoid strenuous activities\nduring peak afternoon heating, and practice extreme caution to\nprevent any sparks or fire starts in these dry, windy conditions. \n\nA surge of moisture will move into the region from the south,\nelevating the risk for showers and thunderstorms across western\nMontana and north-central Idaho, bringing relief from the hot,\ndry, and windy weekend conditions. The initial shower and\nthunderstorm activity will bring mostly cloudy skies and erratic,\ngusty winds due to the very dry air at the surface. By Tuesday\nevening, the atmosphere will moisten enough for rain to reach the\nground. If you have outdoor plans Tuesday through mid-week, plan\naccordingly.",
